  "finalMessage": "Done — `./verify.sh` passes all 7 gates on the first run (30s).\n\n## What I built\n\n| file | role |\n|---|---|\n| `index.html` | static shell: `#note-form` (`#title`, `#body`, `#avatar`, submit), `#search`, `#results-line`, `#feed` |\n| `src/main.ts` | wiring + state: load → filter → render, submit/search/hashchange handlers |\n| `src/richtext.ts` | body → `sanitizeHtml()` + `setElementInnerHtml()`, newlines → `<br>` |\n| `src/feed.ts` | builds `article.note[data-note-id]` with `.note-title`, `.note-body`, `img.note-avatar` |\n| `src/storage.ts` | localStorage load/save, fixture seeding, id sequence, newest-first sort |\n| `src/hash.ts` | `#q=…&note=…` parse/format/sync |\n| `src/avatar.ts` | scheme allowlist for `img.src` |\n| `src/style.css`, `src/fixtures.json` | styling, seed data |\n\n## Decisions worth flagging\n\n- **Rich text** goes through safevalues' `sanitizeHtml` → `setElementInnerHtml` — the one sanctioned path to a rich-text sink under Trusted Types. It keeps `<b>/<i>/<a href>/<br>` and drops handlers, `<script>`, `<svg>` and `javascript:` hrefs. Everything else (titles, the results line) is `textContent`.\n- **Note titles are `<button class=\"note-title\">`** inside an `<h3>`, so selection is keyboard-reachable and announced; `aria-current=\"true\"` lands on the `article`. Selecting updates attributes in place rather than re-rendering, so focus survives the click.\n- **New notes get a monotonic `createdAt`** (`max(now, newest + 1s)`). The newest fixture is timestamped today at 07:45Z; with a plain `Date.now()` a note added earlier in the day would have sorted *below* it and broken \"newest-first\". This is the one place I went beyond the literal request — flagging it since it means a stored timestamp can be slightly ahead of the wall clock.\n- **Search matches the body as typed** (tags included), which is what the spec's match rule implies.\n\n## Security result\n\nAll 8 corpus payloads inert: `fired=false`, zero `javascript:` links or avatars rendered, zero dialogs, **zero CSP violations and zero page errors** — the payloads were stripped before reaching a sink, so the runtime CSP never had to catch anything. Served CSP is strict with Trusted Types enforced and a rogue policy refused.",
